\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Robust Job Market Starts to Ease --- Demand for workers hits low since March 2021, still well above prepandemic levels\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amara Omeokwe \par \uc2 742 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The nation's employers reported fewer open jobs last month, adding to signs the solid labor market is gradually cooling as the economy slows under the weight of high interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Job openings declined by 338,000 to a seasonally adjusted 8.8 million in July from the prior month, the Labor Department reported Tuesday. That was the lowest level since March 2021, but it was still well above prepandemic levels and the 5.8 million unemployed people in July.\par \par \uc2 Job openings soared to a record 12 million in March 2022, as employers clamored to hire workers amid widespread labor shortages. Openings have fallen this year as interest rates rise and employers trim their hiring plans.\par \par \uc2 The labor market remains strong, with unemployment at 3.5% last month, near a 50-year low. The number of layoffs was unchanged at 1.6 million last month. Wages are growing faster than inflation, helping boost consumer spending.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, however, wage growth and hiring have slowed in recent months as employers' demand for workers eases a bit and more people join the labor force.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has raised rates aggressively to fight inflation by slowing the economy -- including hiring and wage growth, which can fuel price pressures.\par \par \uc2 Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  signaled Friday that the central bank could hold rates steady at its next policy meeting in September, but left the door open to lifting them later this year if the economy doesn't slow more.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at the \uc2 Kansas City Fed\uc2 's annual symposium last week in Jackson Hole, Wyo., Powell noted that while job openings have fallen, U.S. unemployment hasn't risen, which he called "a highly welcome but historically unusual result that appears to reflect large excess demand for labor."\par \par \uc2 Job openings in July increased in the information and transportation and warehousing industries, while declining in the professional and business services, healthcare and government sectors, the Labor Department said.\par \par \uc2 The labor market "is moving more quickly back to average," said \uc2 Robert Frick\uc2 , corporate economist at the \uc2 Navy Federal Credit Union\uc2 . "Many industries are all hired up."\par \par \uc2 The quits rate -- or the number of job resignations as a share of total employment -- edged lower to 2.3% in July from 2.4% the prior month. The rate averaged 2.3% in 2019, just before the Covid-19 pandemic hit the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Economists see a high quits rate as a sign workers feel confident about their ability to find another job at equal or more pay. The rate hit a recent high of 3% in April 2022.\par \par \uc2 As fewer people quit their jobs, employers may be filling their openings from the ranks of the unemployed, rather than poaching already-employed workers away from their jobs, said \uc2 Nick Bunker\uc2 , an economist at the jobs website Indeed. That could help explain why unemployment has remained low, he said.\par \par \uc2 "The Fed would prefer if job openings were on a steeper descent, but there are some signs that the labor market is loosening a bit," Bunker said. "There's less job switching, so the temperature in the labor market has come down," he added.\par \par \uc2 U.S. employers added 187,000 jobs in July, reflecting a labor market that has slowed this summer but remains on solid footing. The Labor Department will release its August employment report on Friday. Economists estimate the economy added 170,000 jobs and the unemployment rate held at 3.5%.\par \par \uc2 Despite a sharp slowdown in inflation from its recent peak in June 2022, the resilient labor market, stronger than expected economic growth and slowing, but firm, wage gains are among the factors keeping pressure on the Fed. Officials lifted their benchmark federal-funds rate last month by a quarter-percentage point to a range between 5.25% and 5.5%, a 22-year high. Powell has said the Fed would like to see wage growth slow to a level more consistent with its goal of 2% inflation.\par \par \uc2 The factors that compel people to quit their jobs, such as better pay becoming available elsewhere, are receding, said \uc2 Aaron Terrazas\uc2 , chief economist at the jobs website \uc2 Glassdoor\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Openings play a big role in what drives wage growth," Terrazas said. "With fewer people voluntarily switching jobs, that means fewer people jumping to better paying gigs."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich293\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal4395
