\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: AI Expected to Bring Gains In Output, Jobs Rethink --- Productivity boost may be coming, but some workers will face a reckoning\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Fernandez \par \uc2 927 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 August 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The potential macroeconomic benefits of generative artificial intelligence and machine-learning technologies are staggering. Generative AI, by some estimates, stands to double the rate of U.S. productivity growth after a decade of widespread adoption and add trillions of dollars a year to global economic output.\par \par \uc2 The microeconomic disruption of such game-changing innovation in the workplace is a more complicated story. The technology behind the ChatGPT application will take tasks from millions of employees higher on the wage ladder more than it does from those whose jobs were diminished by factory or warehouse robots in recent decades. So-called knowledge workers and white-collar professionals will feel more pain this time around.\par \par \uc2 Generative AI algorithms can, or are expected to soon, read and summarize into bullet points business, medical and legal documents. Startup AI firms are developing chatbots as well as generators for text, computer code, images, video, design, voice and music. Illustrators, healthcare workers, actors, educators, legal researchers, office workers and drug-company technicians could be the first occupations threatened with this new form of AI.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, the issue is whether displaced skilled employees find comparable work or if they will be consigned to less economically valuable work in the AI-driven economy.\par \par \uc2 "We will not run out of jobs," said David H. Autor, a \uc2 Massachusetts Institute of Technology\uc2  economics professor who studies labor-market impacts and technological change. "It's a question of whether we will have jobs that make use of scarce skills where people are valuable or whether we have jobs that are much more menial."\par \par \uc2 Some professionals already find themselves in conflict with AI. In Hollywood, the actors union is striking in part over the threat of "digital replicas" -- a form of AI -- and the potential that studios could produce entertainment without actors' physical presence or consent, and without compensating them for it.\par \par \uc2 The union says studios could profit from these replicas while the actors have no control over their images or creative output. "We see it coming and there has to be guardrails," said Duncan Crabtree-Ireland, the chief negotiator for \uc2 SAG-Aftra\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jim Weatherall, vice president for data science and AI at drug company \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 , said the company has been using generative AI, or a form of it, for several years. There was a time when a biologist could read all the available scientific literature out there.\par \par \uc2 Generative AI allows researchers to "intelligently interrogate" the information and it is envisioned that it could then go to the next step and suggest untapped areas for developing drugs, Weatherall said.\par \par \uc2 Jack Kokko, chief executive at market-intelligence firm AlphaSense, said his company has layered a generative AI tool on its search engine of financial documents. The new product is called Smart Summaries.\par \par \uc2 The tool can summarize the financial documents in bullet points, "the kind of thing that more junior analysts or business professionals would have done manually," Kokko said.\par \par \uc2 An issue with generative AI is so-called hallucination, with the AI reaching faulty or wrong conclusions. AlphaSense has taken precautions against hallucination by, in part, linking to the underlying data and training the generative AI tool to access trustworthy business content, the company said.\par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  has developed a generative AI tool that enables thousands of employees to query 100,000 internal corporate documents for answers.\par \par \uc2 The bank says the goal of the tool is to free up time for advisers to do more important things, such as talking with clients. They can ask the AI for research on a stock, the benefits of a \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  product or how to open an individual retirement account. The tool reads through the 100,000 documents for text on the topic and summarizes an answer. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  tested the tool on thousands of questions asked of it by employees who rated the answers to help train it.\par \par \uc2 Parsing the tasks of hundreds of occupations for automation potential with generative AI, the \uc2 McKinsey Global Institute\uc2  estimated in a June report that 75% of the value of generative AI could fall in four areas: customer service, marketing and sales, software engineering, and research and development.\par \par \uc2 Generative AI could interact with customers, develop creative content for marketing and sales and draft computer code based on natural-language prompts, the report said.\par \par \uc2 McKinsey's research estimates generative AI could add the equivalent of $2.6 trillion to $4.4 trillion a year globally.\par \par \uc2 In a report in July, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  estimated generative AI could raise annual labor productivity growth in the U.S. by 1.5 percentage points after 10 years of broad adoption. Of U.S. work tasks, 25% could be automated over time with the AI advances, according to the report.\par \par \uc2 Assuming a 40-hour workweek, the 1.5 percentage points would be equivalent to accomplishing an additional 36 minutes of work.\par \par \uc2 "A boost of this size would roughly double the recent pace of U.S. productivity growth, and would be about the same size as the boost that followed the emergence of prior transformative technologies like the electric motor and personal computer," Joseph Briggs, senior global economist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , wrote in the report.\par \par \uc2 The productivity gains of the type that \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  is talking about amounts to $400 billion to $600 billion a year said \uc2 Mark Zandi\uc2 , \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics chief economist.\par \par \uc2 Goldman said the productivity gains wouldn't necessarily be realized over the next 10 years.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich382\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal5730
