\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  Approve Contract With \uc2 UPS\uc2 , Averting Strike Peril\b0\par\par\uc2 By Esther Fung \par \uc2 526 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2  said its members have ratified a new five-year, $30 billion contract with \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2 , ending the prospects of a labor standoff that could have been damaging for U.S. supply chains.\par \par \uc2 UPS\uc2  and union leaders last month agreed to a pact that covers roughly 330,000 package-delivery drivers and package sorters. The prior contract expired July 31.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  said Tuesday that 86.3% of members voted to ratify the contract, which is the highest vote for a contract for \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  at \uc2 UPS\uc2 . The union has said the contract provides raises for new part-time workers as well as current employees.\par \par \uc2 The UPS-Teamsters contract is the largest collective-bargaining agreement involving a private employer in North America. It also covers the majority of \uc2 UPS\uc2 's U.S. workforce, which totaled 443,000 at the end of last year.\par \par \uc2 While negotiations were ongoing, \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  members voted to authorize a strike if a new deal wasn't reached, a move that could have harmed the supply chains of many companies. \uc2 UPS\uc2  moves about 5% of the nation's gross domestic product -- or roughly $3.8 billion of goods every day, according to the \uc2 U.S. Chamber of Commerce\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, \uc2 UPS\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Carol Tome\uc2  said that the new agreement restores labor certainty and addresses the concerns of workers, shareholders and the company. She said by the end of the new contract, the average full-time driver would make $170,000 annually in pay and benefits. She added that \uc2 UPS\uc2  is focused on winning back customers that moved business away from the company due to the threat of a strike.\par \par \uc2 Under the agreement, \uc2 UPS\uc2  would pay new part-time workers a wage of $21 an hour, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  said. Currently, starting part-time hourly wages are $16.20, though it could be higher in places where there is more competition for labor.\par \par \uc2 Existing workers would get a raise of $7.50 an hour over the life of the contract, including a $2.75-an-hour pay bump this year.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UPS\uc2  pact with the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  reflects the changed landscape for transportation workers, many of whom have called for earning better pay and recognition for showing up through the Covid-19 pandemic, when other staffers were able to work remotely.\par \par \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  pilots on Monday ratified a new contract that would boost wages by more than 40% over its four-year term after the carrier agreed to sweeten terms to match a rival's deal.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations between other groups of transportation workers haven't gone as smoothly.\par \par \uc2 Pilots at \uc2 FedEx\uc2  rejected a tentative agreement in July. The cargo airline pilots will remain paid under the terms of the existing contract for now. The union and the company will go back to the negotiating table for a new agreement.\par \par \uc2 Protracted labor negotiations this year have irked businesses and caused some supply-chain disruptions. The union representing auto workers, which is negotiating a new four-year contract, said last week that its negotiations with the Detroit automakers have been sluggish.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230823ej8n0001n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
89
