\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Pay for Tipped Staff Stirs Fight in Chicago\b0\par\par\uc2 By Heather Haddon \par \uc2 275 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 August 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO -- A national battle over tipping has come to one of America's culinary capitals.\par \par \uc2 Servers, restaurant owners and lawmakers in Chicago are debating whether waitstaff and other restaurant workers should be able to earn the city's minimum wage of $15.80 for larger businesses.\par \par \uc2 Right now, their wage isn't that cut and dried. Under the so-called tipped wage system, in Illinois and other states, employers can pay below minimum wage to workers who also earn tips.\par \par \uc2 In Chicago, large businesses pay servers, bartenders and other tipped employees a minimum $9.48 an hour -- with tips making up the difference. If their tips don't push their hourly pay over the $15.80 minimum, their employer must make up the difference.\par \par \uc2 Activists are pushing to eliminate the decades-old system in Chicago, and elsewhere. They say it relies on diners' goodwill to subsidize workers' wages -- and that sit-down restaurant staff should be able to earn the same minimum pay as other workers.\par \par \uc2 Some restaurant owners say a change to the current system would upend their businesses as they are recovering from inflation and pandemic-related sales losses. They say it will force them to hike menu prices for diners, and potentially reduce staff to offset the higher wages.\par \par \uc2 Some Chicago restaurant owners have voluntarily started paying a set wage across staff and said it is helping them attract employees. Joe Frillman eliminated the tipped wage and asked for a 25% gratuity on dine-in checks instead of tips and fees at his Daisies restaurant in late 2020.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230821ej8l0001k\par }\page {\par\fs20{