\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Strike Spotlights Side Gigs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Esther Zuckerman \par \uc2 669 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 August 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When actor Adam Shapiro, known for his work in shows like "The Bear" and \uc2 Netflix\uc2 's "Never Have I Ever," hits the \uc2 SAG-Aftra\uc2  picket lines, he often hears familiar shouts directed at him from fellow strikers: "Pretzels!"\par \par \uc2 That's because Shapiro's presence on the Los Angeles picket lines means there are likely free, Philadelphia-style soft pretzels around from his company Shappy Pretzel Co. "Just never in a million years would I think that people would be yelling pretzels at me," he said.\par \par \uc2 As soon as the Writers Guild of America went on strike in May, Shapiro was providing pretzels. Now that \uc2 SAG-Aftra\uc2 , a group that Shapiro is a part of, joined them, demand has only grown. "I was like, I'm going to go picket anyway, I might as well show up with pretzels," he said.\par \par \uc2 With Hollywood shut down amid a fight between actors and writers and the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  some people in the industry have been turning to their food side projects to bring sustenance to the protesters. Much of the food is donated by the purveyors, or it's offered through sponsorships from the famous people on strike.\par \par \uc2 Actor Paul Cassell, who has appeared on shows like "Gilmore Girls" and "Masters of Sex," has been distributing the cookies he makes with Paul Cassell Bakes. He started pursuing baking as a business a little over a year and a half ago, he said. It was a "no-brainer" to bring cookies with him to the picket lines, which he pays for out of pocket for his "union brothers and sisters." The sweets bring smiles in the midst of what is, at its core, a serious fight over their contracts.\par \par \uc2 Even more famous actors' companies have been spotted handing out supplies, like the cactus-water drink Caliwater, founded by "High School Musical" star Vanessa Hudgens and actor Oliver Trevena. "I was out on the picket line and it was extremely hot," Hudgens said via email. "When I planned to go out the next time I wanted not only to support by showing up, but also offer what I could to support the actors on the line." The Caliwater deliveries to pickets are coordinated via a \uc2 SAG\uc2  representative, according to a publicist for Hudgens.\par \par \uc2 Andrea Alba-von Buren and Olivier Alerte began Dean's Coffee as a way to make money when their work dried up because of the strikes. Alba-von Buren, having worked as a writer's production assistant, knew how much writers loved iced coffee. "I had to get that every day," she said.\par \par \uc2 They started out just selling drinks, but soon showrunner James Stoteraux, with whom Alba-von Buren worked with on the CW show "Gotham Knights," offered to send her enough money so she and Alerte could give away coffee.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, Shapiro and his wife Katie Lowes, an actress who has been on shows like "Scandal," attended the Oscars because host \uc2 Jimmy Kimmel\uc2  included the pretzels in snack boxes that were distributed to audience members. "It's almost not strange to go from the Oscars to the picket line within a couple of months, because that's what it's like being an actor," said Shapiro.\par \par \uc2 Shappy Pretzel started after Shapiro began experimenting with making the treat from his hometown of Abington, Pa., outside of Philadelphia during the pandemic. What started in his home, now operates out of Los Angeles' Diamond Bakery and has 10 people on staff.Shappy's business involves on-set catering, so Shapiro wanted to make sure his employees stayed busy as productions halted. "At least my bakers are still baking," he said.\par \par \uc2 After initially donating the pretzels himself, he began receiving DMs from showrunners, actors, and producers asking to sponsor Shappy Pretzel drops. Late-night hosts including Kimmel, Stephen Colbert, Jimmy Fallon, Seth Meyers and John Oliver send "like 1,000 pretzels to the writers every couple of weeks."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230815ej8f00013\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
