\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Your Marriage Needs a CFO And a COO --- Couples take a page out of the corporate playbook to split household tasks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Julia Carpenter \par \uc2 1,045 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 August 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Couples who struggle with dividing household chores are taking a page out of the corporate playbook to better organize their finances and day-to-day operations.\par \par \uc2 Some partners adopt the roles of chief financial officer and chief operating officer to split their household duties. Budgeting for the family, balancing accounts, managing investments -- that is the CFO's job. Booking appointments, organizing the calendar and making sure the trains run on time -- that is the COO's territory.\par \par \uc2 Like in a Fortune 500 company, these two roles ideally work together as a well-oiled leadership team. For couples who are restricting their budgets and struggling with financial uncertainty, dividing the labor can make managing household tasks more efficient and lessen financial strife in a relationship, researchers and financial therapists said. Some romantic partners who practice this setup have found it easier to reach their financial goals.\par \par \uc2 "At the core, there does need to be this established level of agreement between couples about 'How are we going to get through daily life and accomplish all the tasks it takes to get to the finish line successfully every day?'" said Megan Ford, financial therapist and clinic director at the University of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Omar Ramirez, a 37-year-old startup founder in Los Angeles, and his wife, Marissa Ramirez, a 38-year-old stay-at-home mom, first discussed the CFO-COO approach when they began home-schooling their two small children.\par \par \uc2 Dividing the labor -- Omar taking the role of CFO and Marissa that of the COO -- freed up more time in their days. Each felt a sense of purpose in knowing they accomplished their tasks and trusted the other person to do theirs, the Ramirezes said.\par \par \uc2 In the corporate world, specialization is the key to getting things done efficiently. This specialized approach to dividing chores can help couples achieve the same goals, said Emily Oster, economics professor at \uc2 Brown University\uc2  and author of "The Family Firm," a book on data-driven parenting.\par \par \uc2 Talking about who wants to adopt the CFO duties and who prefers to take on the COO role can allow partners to operate to the best of their ability, within their own sphere of control, Oster said.\par \par \uc2 There isn't always a fair breakdown of household tasks, and gendered assumptions of what constitutes "men's work" and "women's work" remain sticky for many heterosexual couples. Even as women's financial contributions increase, men still spend more time on paid work and leisure as their wives take on more of the housework and caregiving responsibilities, according to data from \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stepping into the CFO and COO roles can also help both partners achieve a mind-set that embraces accountability and builds trust, two traits already lauded in the business world, said Eve Rodsky, lawyer and author of "Fair Play," a book about dividing housework equitably.\par \par \uc2 When both partners know their roles, they feel a greater sense of autonomy within the relationship.\par \par \uc2 "I want people to feel they have agency in their own home, to not feel decision fatigue, to feel like they can trust one another and have a little bit of that mental load relieved," Rodsky said.\par \par \uc2 The CFO-COO model is only helpful if both people are working together as a leadership team, not alone in separate lanes, Oster said.\par \par \uc2 "That is never supposed to be a one-time conversation," Oster said of the who-does-what talk.\par \par \uc2 Expressing gratitude for the work each partner takes on also goes a long way toward minimizing conflict within such a CFO-COO arrangement.\par \par \uc2 "If you are in a COO role, don't forget to acknowledge and express appreciation to your partner for what they are managing. And if your nose is in the spreadsheet all the time, you can be confronted with some stressful events, and that can lead to you feeling isolated," said Ford, the financial therapist.\par \par \uc2 Within their own roles, both partners must have the ability to make judgments and big moves, Oster said. In some cases, the CFO-COO division of labor fails when one person is making big decisions and the other is merely implementing them, playing second-in-command.\par \par \uc2 "They don't really feel they're doing it together," Oster said. "That doesn't work well if you are trying to be partners."\par \par \uc2 Make sure you have a contingency plan in case one partner falls ill, the relationship ends, an emergency hits or either the CFO or COO needs support. This team dynamic fails if catastrophe strikes and neither partner is able to step in.\par \par \uc2 For Jaimie Walters, a 36-year-old government worker in Fayetteville, N.C., playing the CFO role helps to alleviate her money anxiety. As a child, she grew up with little financial stability, and after her first marriage ended, Walters said building a nest egg for her family became a priority.\par \par \uc2 Now, she said she prefers to be the partner who manages the savings accounts and keeps track of the family financial goals. Her husband, meanwhile, takes the lead on home-improvement projects and the children's after-school activities.\par \par \uc2 "For me, knowing there is a positive balance in that bank account is already giving my kids what I didn't have for most of my childhood," she said.\par \par \uc2 Together, Jaimie and her husband, Scott Walters, have already made progress in paying off debt and building up their household-emergency fund. The evolution of their chores distribution also taught Jaimie that playing the role of CFO isn't always solitary.\par \par \uc2 "I knew that it had to be a partnership," she said.\par \par \uc2 The Ramirezes, who adopted the CFO-COO approach three years ago, said they are open to their roles shifting over time. Recently, Omar said he noticed that his wife was taking on a greater share of the financial labor, such as managing their school-supplies budget. At the same time, he assumed more duties at home, including teaching science and math to his children.\par \par \uc2 "Because our roles are so fluid, we see ourselves as partners, and we see ourselves doing this thing called life together," he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230815ej8f00008\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
