\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Major Studios Resume Talks With Writers --- Latest dialogue shows signs of progress but big hurdles remain\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Flint \par \uc2 696 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 August 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Negotiations between striking television and movie writers and major studios have resumed, with signs of progress on certain issues, giving the entertainment industry a glimmer of hope amid the monthslong labor dispute.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  (\uc2 AMPTP\uc2 ), which represents major content providers including \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2 ., \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  and others, submitted a new proposal that the Writers Guild of America said it is reviewing and will respond to this week.\par \par \uc2 The proposal from the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  is the first major movement between the two sides since the WGA went on strike on May 2. The Screen Actors Guild joined writers on the picket lines last month, and the strikes have ground Hollywood to a halt.\par \par \uc2 The latest proposal from the studios addresses some concerns of the WGA, including its desire for a requirement that television shows be staffed with a minimum number of writers, people close to the discussions said. Staffing requirements have previously been a nonstarter for the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2 , which viewed any such rule as a hiring quota at odds with the creative process.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  didn't publicly release details of its proposal, and it is unclear if it will satisfy the WGA. The writers union has accused production companies of reducing the role of writers in the making of television shows by ordering fewer episodes and allowing fewer writers to stay with a show through the postproduction process.\par \par \uc2 The new \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  proposal also addresses the use of artificial intelligence in film and TV production, which has been another sticking point, the people close to the discussions said. There is concern among writers and actors that generative-AI tools could eliminate jobs and result in poor content.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  has proposed guidelines regarding the use of AI but the WGA and Screen Actors Guild have felt they aren't strong enough.\par \par \uc2 If a deal with the WGA is reached, the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  will turn its attention to actors. The dual strike, the first since 1960, has divided Hollywood and had a dramatic effect on the Los Angeles economy. All production has been shut down, meaning that everyone who works on scripted content, from makeup artists to lighting experts, is out of work.\par \par \uc2 Dining hot spots that are usually full of executives, agents and actors are now often half full and a town known for its hustle and bustle has ground to a halt. Instead of making deals and content, writers and actors are picketing studios every day and calling out CEOs overexecutive compensation and greed.\par \par \uc2 Conversely, some studio chiefs have privately grumbled that the entertainment industry is struggling as it tries to pivot away from the traditional movie and television business to embrace streaming, which has turned into a financial sinkhole for several of the industry's big players.\par \par \uc2 Last month, \uc2 Disney\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Robert Iger\uc2  made waves during a CNBC interview in which he said some of the creative community's demands weren't realistic, given the state of the industry. \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2  and other entertainment giants have made significant staff reductions, largely because of the struggle to find an economic model that can sustain the rising costs of content creation.\par \par \uc2 There are other issues to be settled between the studios and writers, including increases in residuals, which are the royalties writers receive for television shows and movies. The \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  has expressed a willingness to increase royalties, particularly from streaming services.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 AMPTP\uc2 's recent deal with the \uc2 Directors Guild of America\uc2  could provide a road map to resolving the residual issue. Under terms of that pact, directors received an increase of more than 20% in royalties from streaming services, much of it coming from a 76% increase in residuals from the international operations of streaming services.\par \par \uc2 A WGA spokesman didn't respond to a request for comment. Last week, the WGA said it would refrain from providing comments on talks "until there is something of significance to report, or unless management uses the media or industry surrogates to try to influence the narrative."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230815ej8f0000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
