\par\par \b \uc2 Banking & Finance: Natural Gas Jolted by Australian Labor Dispute\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stuart Condie \par \uc2 694 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 August 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY -- A dispute between Australian energy companies and their workers has natural-gas buyers bracing for a possible curtailment of supply from one of the world's largest exporters of the fuel.\par \par \uc2 The threat of disruptions is particularly worrisome for Europe, which is heading into its second winter since the Ukraine war cut gas supplies from Russia. Most of Australia's supply is committed to Asia, so a sudden drop in LNG exports could push European and Asian buyers into a bidding war for gas from elsewhere, including from the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Europe's energy crisis last year elevated the importance of stability of energy supply from Australia, which rivals the U.S. and Qatar as one of the world's biggest LNG exporters. Gas futures in the U.S. and Europe shot up Wednesday after workers at Australian producer \uc2 Woodside Energy\uc2  voted in favor of potential industrial action that could include strikes. But prices eased Thursday after U.S. energy regulators reported that domestic stockpiles grew last week by more than analysts had expected.\par \par \uc2 Workers at three LNG platforms off the coast of Western Australia voted Wednesday to take action after months of unresolved negotiations over wages and working conditions. Union officials say workers at \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's Gorgon and Wheatstone facilities could also vote on potential action next week. Under Australian employment law, workers can stop work within 30 days of such a vote providing they give employers seven days' notice.\par \par \uc2 "They are well aware of the hundreds of millions of dollars these companies will lose if protected industrial action slows exports of Australian gas. It's in everyone's best interest to get back to what our members know best, exporting quality Australian gas to the world," said Brad Gandy, an official with the \uc2 Australian Workers' Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Vivek Dhar, an analyst at \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 , said the export facilities in Australia that could be affected by strike action accounted for 12% of global LNG exports last year. "With the northern winter season fast approaching, any sustained outage at these facilities threatens to meaningfully tighten traded gas markets," Dhar said. "The winter season in the Northern Hemisphere typically sees the largest increase in gas usage in the year."\par \par \uc2 European countries have become reliant on the supercooled fuel since Russia cut off most of the gas it used to pump to the continent and the labor dispute in Australia swiftly reverberated through global markets.\par \par \uc2 Benchmark European natural-gas prices surged 28% Wednesday. Prices retreated some Thursday, but remained about 19% higher than on Tuesday. U.S. gas futures ended Thursday 6.6% lower at $2.763 per million British thermal units, down 69% from a year ago, when Europe's scramble for fuel pushed prices to shale-era highs and helped stoke broad inflation. U.S. futures had climbed 6.6% on Wednesday following the \uc2 Woodside\uc2  ballot.\par \par \uc2 Prices for LNG exported from the U.S. Gulf Coast rose, as did the cost of transporting the fuel to Europe or Asia, according to data provider \uc2 Argus Media\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Traders said the initial leap in European prices was exacerbated because many market players had bet on further price declines. When the market moved against them, some cut losses by buying back gas they had sold, pushing prices yet higher. Hedging trades related to the options market boosted European prices, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Prices in Europe are still far lower than their all-time highs from just under a year ago after Moscow first slashed natural-gas flows. Yet a strike lasting beyond October could push Asian LNG and European natural-gas prices for the Northern Hemisphere winter to almost double from those seen early this week, \uc2 Citi\uc2  analysts said in a note.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 Woodside\uc2  said that the producer had reached an in-principle agreement with workers on a number of issues and that talks were progressing positively. \uc2 Woodside\uc2 , Australia's seventh-largest company by market capitalization, said it has contingency plans in case of disruption.\par \par \uc2 Chevron\uc2  didn't immediately respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Joe Wallace and Ryan Dezember contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich451\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal6765
