\par\par \b \uc2 Truckers, Pilots Turn Up the Heat --- Transportation industry faces heightened activism from labor unions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Esther Fung \par \uc2 772 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Pilots can go on strike under federal law only after certain conditions are met. A Business & Finance article on Monday about labor activism in the transportation industry incorrectly said FedEx pilots aren't allowed to strike under federal law.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ Aug. 8, 2023)\par \par \uc2 (END)\par \par \uc2 Surging demands on transportation workers are fueling labor standoffs at companies critical to U.S. supply chains.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , which represents drivers at trucking company Yellow, threatened a strike shortly before the company shut down July 30. \uc2 FedEx\uc2  pilots recently rejected a new labor contract promising a roughly 30% raise. Port workers in Canada staged walkouts earlier this year, and dockworkers at West Coast ports slowed work at container terminals.\par \par \uc2 The renewed activism follows the Covid-19 pandemic, when demands on train operators, package carriers, and truckers surged to handle a deluge of business.\par \par \uc2 Transportation workers said they are entitled to a larger share of the corporate profit generated during the pandemic and better pay and recognition for showing up through the health crisis when other staff was able to work remotely.\par \par \uc2 "We know what we've sacrificed to make certain that goods and services are provided," \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  General President Sean O'Brien said at a rally in Atlanta on July 22, days before a tentative agreement with \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  was reached and amid the Yellow threats. "Now is our time to be rewarded."\par \par \uc2 Employers say they are offering outsize wage increases and changing their operations to improve conditions for workers. \uc2 UPS\uc2  got rid of a lower-paid weekend driver role and agreed to ensure that delivery vehicles have adequate cooling equipment for drivers. Railroads are promising more predictable work schedules so workers can plan and attend family activities on their days off.\par \par \uc2 Recent labor standoffs have irked businesses and, in some cases, resulted in snafus in the movement of goods around the country. Souring relations between the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  and Yellow prompted several companies to pull their business.\par \par \uc2 The slowdown of work at West Coast ports caused some ships to take a longer route to dock at ports along the East Coast instead. To prevent hazardous and other sensitive material from being left unattended in the event of a labor stoppage, railroad operators imposed restrictions on handling some commodities days before a strike deadline. Industry groups have warned about the damage that potential work stoppages could have on supply chains.\par \par \uc2 Lynn Kelley, a business consultant who recently published a book, said she was worried about a possible \uc2 UPS\uc2  strike affecting the book's distribution, including large bulk shipments for some events.\par \par \uc2 "There might have been some delays," said Kelley, adding that her book is on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , which handles shipping. "My concern is even if \uc2 Amazon\uc2  does use an alternative to \uc2 UPS\uc2 , everything will be slow because there's not a lot of extra capacity."\par \par \uc2 Unions have been pushing companies to improve on-the-job conditions. After lobbying for it last year, major freight railroads have started offering short-term paid sick leave -- in some cases for the first time -- to union workers to ensure ample staffing and service levels. "What we found over the pandemic -- and I think you see this throughout all of society -- is that those quality-of-life issues become more and more important to folks," said \uc2 Alan Shaw\uc2 , chief executive officer of transportation company \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  representatives said that the labor pact with \uc2 UPS\uc2  included provisions that would stop the installation of driver-facing cameras. The changes were in response to concerns that their work is increasingly measured against a stopwatch and that drivers were being surveilled for minor infractions, union employees and officials said.\par \par \uc2 "Our focus is on safety, and not on surveillance or discipline," a \uc2 UPS\uc2  spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Pilots at \uc2 FedEx\uc2  recently surprised industry observers by voting down a union-brokered deal that would have raised their wages about 30% by 2028. Some pilots said that they saw their counterparts at passenger airlines get higher wages in recent weeks, and that they were also worried about insufficient job protections.\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  said the voting results will have no impact on service levels. The \uc2 Air Line Pilots Association, International\uc2 , which represents \uc2 FedEx\uc2  pilots, and \uc2 FedEx\uc2  will return to the bargaining table with mediators from the \uc2 National Mediation Board\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  pilots will continue working under the terms of the existing contract and aren't allowed to strike under federal law. Workers in some railroad unions last year voted against a tentative labor deal, but Congress and President Biden stepped in to impose a settlement.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230807ej870001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
