\par\par \b \uc2 Slower Hiring Offers the Fed Wiggle Room\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gabriel T. Rubin and Nick Timiraos \par \uc2 827 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employers slowed their hiring this summer, adding to signs the economy is gradually cooling and easing pressure for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise interest rates at its next meeting.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy added 187,000 jobs in July, a still solid increase nearly matching June's downwardly revised 185,000 gain, the Labor Department said Friday. Those figures are down significantly from a year earlier and below last year's average employment growth of about 400,000 a month.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate fell to 3.5% last month from 3.6% in June, remaining near a half-century low. Wages continued to rise briskly, with average hourly earnings growing 4.4% in July from a year earlier, the same rate as in June -- down from last year but well above the prepandemic pace.\par \par \uc2 Stocks initially climbed after the jobs data was released Friday morning, but closed lower. The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 150.27 points, or 0.4%. The broader S&P 500 Index was down 0.53% and the tech-heavy Nasdaq declined 0.36%.\par \par \uc2 "The job market is still on a slow but steady path towards a soft landing," \uc2 Glassdoor\uc2 's lead economist, Daniel Zhao, said, referring to the outcome where inflation returns to the Fed's 2% target without a recession and deep job losses.\par \par \uc2 Friday's employment report would allow the Fed to hold rates steady at its Sept. 19-20 meeting if a recent slowdown in inflation continues. The report on its own is unlikely to tip the scales: Officials will get additional hiring data for August and inflation readings for July and August before the meeting.\par \par \uc2 Atlanta Fed\uc2  President \uc2 Raphael Bostic\uc2  said this week he didn't think officials needed to keep raising rates. He said the hiring trend was more important than any particular month-to-month changes.\par \par \uc2 "I've never expected this to move in a straight line," Bostic told reporters on Tuesday. "I figured there'd be bumps up and down."\par \par \uc2 Fed officials have said they expect job growth to slow partly because of their efforts to reduce inflation. "We have to be honest about the historical record, which does suggest that when central banks go in and slow the economy to bring down inflation, the result tends to be some softening in labor market conditions," Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said on July 26.\par \par \uc2 Adding to signs of cooling labor demand, Americans also worked less in July, with average weekly hours ticking down slightly to match the lowest level since April 2020.\par \par \uc2 Job openings decreased in June and workers quit jobs at a slower rate, suggesting lower confidence they could land new positions.\par \par \uc2 Wilson Gardner, 31, said he was laid off in May from his product manager position at \uc2 Xometry\uc2 , an online marketplace that serves industrial manufacturers. He initially applied for similar tech jobs. That left him frustrated: It was hard to stand out in a sea of laid-off workers.\par \par \uc2 "Those jobs get posted and 30 minutes later there's 500, 600 applicants," Gardner said. He pivoted to a more skill-based approach to job-hunting, reasoning that there would likely be more opportunities, including locally in Lexington, Ky., in fields such as renewable energy and the automotive industry. He landed several interviews and is hoping for an offer soon.\par \par \uc2 Low unemployment allows workers to still command historically high pay raises. While pay gains are down from a pandemic peak, average hourly earnings have grown at least 4% from a year earlier for two straight years. Consumer inflation was 3% in June.\par \par \uc2 Workers in construction and manufacturing received larger raises on average. The \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  is seeking a 40% pay increase in the next labor contract.\par \par \uc2 In July, retail and transportation employees saw only tepid gains. Yellow, one of the oldest and biggest U.S. trucking businesses, shut down operations Sunday, threatening nearly 30,000 workers' jobs. Those losses aren't reflected in the July report.\par \par \uc2 Recent wage gains exceed a rate economists believe lines up with low, stable inflation. Fed officials would likely see 3.5% annual wage growth as consistent with inflation near their target, assuming that worker productivity grows modestly.\par \par \uc2 "We do expect to see some further moderation in wage growth because of the fact that you see less labor demand," said \uc2 Lydia Boussour\uc2 , senior economist at \uc2 EY\uc2 -Parthenon.\par \par \uc2 Restaurants, hotels, recreation and similar businesses helped drive job growth during much of the recovery. More recently, such hiring has slowed.\par \par \uc2 Alys Beach, a resort in northwest Florida, is still adding staff, and filling jobs more easily than a year ago. The 158-acre property is looking to hire for about 20 positions as it gears up for a busy Labor Day weekend. To fill vacancies, the resort hosts regular job fairs that offer prospective employees a $1,000 signing bonus.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Christian Robles contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw539\pich480\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8085\pichgoal7200
