\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Software Firm to Go Private at $6.5 Billion --- \uc2 New Relic\uc2  strikes deal with private- equity shops \uc2 TPG\uc2 , \uc2 Francisco Partners\b0\par\par\uc2 By Robb M. Stewart and Maria Armental \par \uc2 412 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 August 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Private-equity firms \uc2 Francisco Partners\uc2  and \uc2 TPG\uc2  have agreed to buy \uc2 New Relic\uc2  in a deal that values the software company at about $6.5 billion.\par \par \uc2 The transaction for $87 a share in cash would take San Francisco-based \uc2 New Relic\uc2  off the public markets roughly nine years after it made its debut at $23 a share.\par \par \uc2 New Relic\uc2  shares rose 13.4% in trading Monday, to close at $83.97. The stock touched a record $125.97 in November 2021, but by May 2022, the shares traded for less than $45 each.\par \par \uc2 Take-privates have been a popular strategy for private-equity firms, even in a period of depressed deal making. Last year, the value of take-private deals by U.S. private-equity firms reached $207.1 billion, the highest level in 15 years, according to analytics provider \uc2 PitchBook Data\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While the number of deals this year has kept up with last year's pace, with 43 take-private transactions announced or completed by June 15, the total value involved contracted sharply, dropping to just under $90 billion from $147.2 billion, PitchBook said. And the median deal value has dropped to $482.9 million this year, which is less than half of last year's $1.7 billion level, PitchBook said.\par \par \uc2 As for the \uc2 New Relic\uc2  transaction, the company's directors and major shareholders, who include founder and Executive Chairman \uc2 Lew Cirne\uc2 , \uc2 Jana Partners\uc2  and HMI Capital Management, support the deal, \uc2 New Relic\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The transaction is expected to close late this year or early in 2024, subject to the approval of shareholders and regulatory clearances. \uc2 TPG\uc2  is investing through its \uc2 TPG Capital\uc2  buyout strategy.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal in May reported that the buyout firms teamed up to negotiate a deal with \uc2 New Relic\uc2 , which provides observability software that lets owners of software applications and websites track the performance of their products and services.\par \par \uc2 Francisco Partners\uc2  and \uc2 TPG\uc2  have worked together on other deals. In 2021, the firms acquired cloud-based software business \uc2 Boomi\uc2  from \uc2 Dell Technologies\uc2  for $4 billion including debt.\par \par \uc2 In early July, \uc2 TPG\uc2  agreed to buy \uc2 Forcepoint\uc2 's government cybersecurity business from \uc2 Francisco Partners\uc2  for $2.45 billion. Francisco will retain a minority stake in the unit and continue to own and manage \uc2 Forcepoint\uc2 's commercial cybersecurity business as a separate operation.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230801ej810001k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
