\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Key Age Group Back to Work in Droves\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jeffrey Sparshott \par \uc2 764 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 July 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The core of the American labor force is back.\par \par \uc2 Americans between 25 and 54 years of age are either employed or looking for jobs at rates not seen in two decades, a trend helping counter the exodus of older baby boomers from the workforce.\par \par \uc2 Economists define that age range as in their prime working years -- when most Americans are done with their formal education, aren't ready to retire and tend to be most attached to the labor force.\par \par \uc2 In the first months of the pandemic, nearly four million prime-age workers left the labor market, pushing participation in early 2020 to the lowest level since 1983 -- before women had become as much of a force in the workplace. Prime-age workers now exceed prepandemic levels by almost 2.2 million.\par \par \uc2 That growth is taking a little heat out of the job market and could help the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's efforts to tamp down inflation by keeping wage growth in check.\par \par \uc2 The resurgence of midcareer workers is driven by women taking jobs.\par \par \uc2 The labor-force participation rate for prime-age women was the highest on record, 77.8%, in June. That was well above the 73.5% in April 2020.\par \par \uc2 Men, however, tend to be employed at higher rates. The overall prime-age participation rate rose in June to 83.5%, the highest since 2002.\par \par \uc2 The big draw: a tight labor market. The unemployment rate has hovered near a half-century low for more than a year, and job openings outnumber the ranks of unemployed. Employers can't be as choosy or selective, William Rodgers, vice president and director of the Institute for Economic Equity at the \uc2 St. Louis Fed\uc2 , said earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Employers "are more apt to be willing to work with candidates -- in this case it's working with moms, or parents in general," he said.\par \par \uc2 "Tight labor markets can help to punish those who discriminate in hiring and compensation," Rodgers said.\par \par \uc2 Other factors are also at play. Women aren't having as many children -- there were about 3.66 million births in 2022, 655,000 fewer than the peak in 2007 -- so child-care responsibilities have decreased.\par \par \uc2 Julia Pollak\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2 , said it is possible for women's participation to rise further if employers adopt or the government requires additional family-friendly policies.\par \par \uc2 U.S. female participation lags behind that of other industrialized economies in part because of the cost of child care, which is subsidized elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Employers raised wages, offered employees more flexibility and improved benefits in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Average wage gains remain elevated this year and have recently surpassed inflation. And Americans are logging more hours of work from home than they did before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Employer recruitment efforts helped offset some broader demographic shifts, including an aging population and rise in retirements. The Covid-19 pandemic pushed many out of the workforce, and some older workers haven't returned, particularly those over 65.\par \par \uc2 Much of the decline in the overall participation rate was anticipated as baby boomers aged out of the workforce, but the rise in prime-age workers meant the drop wasn't as steep.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  in January 2020, just before the pandemic hit, forecast the overall participation rate to deteriorate steadily through the 2020s, moving down to 62.4% in the second quarter of this year\par \par \uc2 Instead, the rate was a couple of ticks higher in June at 62.6%, supported by prime-age workers.\par \par \uc2 "It seems like there is almost no cap on the supply of workers, only a speed limit on how fast we can bring them in," Pollak said, referring to both rising prime-age participation and an influx of immigrants into the workforce.\par \par \uc2 There are concerns that the Fed's campaign to bring down inflation through higher interest rates will cause unemployment to rise too much and push some of the most vulnerable workers back to the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 The median forecast among Fed officials shows the unemployment rate rising to 4.1% by the end of this year and 4.5% next year from 3.6% in June.\par \par \uc2 Labor-force participation tends to be cyclical, rising when the economy is strong and falling during downturns. A weaker labor market combined with structural barriers to employment could cap further gains.\par \par \uc2 With "current strength of labor demand set to fade, further progress from here will probably be more gradual," \uc2 Andrew Hunter\uc2 , deputy chief U.S. economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , said in a research note.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw480\pich498\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal7200\pichgoal7470
