\par\par \b \uc2 Yellow Gets Reprieve on Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Page \par \uc2 230 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 July 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  pulled back its threat of a strike this week at financially ailing trucker Yellow, averting an action the company had said would send the business into liquidation.\par \par \uc2 The union said Sunday that it withdrew plans for the walkout, which could have started as soon as Monday, after a pension fund agreed to continue to extend health benefits to unionized workers at Yellow and a sister company and would give Yellow another 30 days to make a missed payment to the fund.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2  said the agreement was reached late Sunday in talks with the Central States Health and Welfare Fund.\par \par \uc2 Yellow officials had no immediate comment on the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  announcement. The Central States fund couldn't be reached for comment.\par \par \uc2 Nashville, Tenn.-based Yellow, one of the largest trucking companies in the U.S. and a major service provider to the Defense Department, has sought to delay payments totaling $50 million to Central States as the company copes with a heavy debt burden and falling cash reserves.\par \par \uc2 Yellow employs about 30,000 workers, including about 22,000 union members.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in Kansas City, Mo., on Friday rejected Yellow's request for an order blocking a strike after telling the court a walkout would send the 99-year-old company into chapter 7 liquidation proceedings.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230724ej7o0001f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
