\par\par \b \uc2 Markets & Finance: London's Canary Wharf Gets Brunt Of Real Estate's Remote-Work Pain\b0\par\par\uc2 By Humza Jilani \par \uc2 473 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LONDON -- Three decades ago, London remade a derelict shipping yard at \uc2 Canary Wharf\uc2  into a forest of glass-and-concrete skyscrapers in a bid to mimic U.S. financial hubs.\par \par \uc2 Now the 128-acre banking district east of central London is suffering a problem also plaguing U.S. cities: emptying office buildings.\par \par \uc2 Last month, \uc2 HSBC Holdings\uc2 , the U.K.'s largest financial firm, said it was leaving its 1.1-million-square-foot headquarters, known as the HSBC Tower, for a smaller building in central London. The move followed a decision by law firm \uc2 Clifford Chance\uc2  to relocate to central London and major office-space downsizings by \uc2 Barclays\uc2  and \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 , among others.\par \par \uc2 Already, \uc2 Canary Wharf\uc2  and its surrounding area have an availability rate of 17.1%, roughly the size of an empty Empire State Building, compared with 10.7% for central London, according to data provided by \uc2 UBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bonds for \uc2 Canary Wharf Group\uc2  -- the company that owns most of the buildings in the area -- are trading at a deep discount, with yields over 16%. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  lowered its credit rating to junk last month.\par \par \uc2 The troubles at \uc2 Canary Wharf\uc2  show how the rapid rise of remote work has reverberated unevenly across global property markets. While the hollowing out of skyscrapers has become a familiar theme in U.S. cities since the pandemic, Europe's office market has held up relatively well, as workers have been far more eager to return to the office.\par \par \uc2 But London has some problems that are familiar to U.S. real estate. The return-to-office rate for London stood at 65% in February, a figure that put it between New York City, which stood at 49%, and Paris, which was at 85%, according to \uc2 JLL\uc2 , a property-services company.\par \par \uc2 Canary Wharf\uc2  has caught the brunt of the problems in London's office market.\par \par \uc2 Work from home and the cost of upgrading old office space to meet environmental regulations "puts Canary Wharf at a disadvantage," said Zachary Gauge, head of European real-estate research at \uc2 UBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, with most of the district held by \uc2 Canary Wharf Group\uc2  -- a joint venture between Qatar's wealth fund and private-equity giant \uc2 Brookfield\uc2  -- or by the Qatari fund directly, the development has space for long-term planning. "The \uc2 Canary Wharf Group\uc2  is very good at making its own weather," said Tony Travers, who directs the \uc2 London School of Economics\uc2 ' London center.\par \par \uc2 Shobi Khan\uc2 , \uc2 Canary Wharf Group\uc2 's chief executive, has outlined a plan for a "Canary Wharf 3.0" that would thrive on residential rents, entertainment offerings and biotech.The group plans to construct a 750,000-square-foot life-sciences center, which it says will be the largest commercial lab in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230713ej7d0000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
