\par\par \uc2 The Property Report\par \b \uc2 Work From Home Mutes Recovery In the Market for Senior Living\b0\par\par\uc2 By Will Parker \par \uc2 562 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Remote work stung the office market. Some analysts wonder if it is hurting senior housing, too.\par \par \uc2 The occupancy rate for senior-housing facilities was 83.4% in the second quarter of 2023. That is up from the pandemic bottom but still below the 87.1% rate in the first quarter of 2020, said the National Investment Center for Seniors Housing & Care, despite growth in the senior population and the easing of Covid-19 protocols that once made visiting family at these properties more difficult.\par \par \uc2 Since the supply of new senior housing buildings is relatively low, analysts point to a lack of demand. One possibility: Remote and hybrid work schedules are keeping many older Americans from moving into senior-living communities.\par \par \uc2 When more adults began working remotely during the pandemic, they were able to more easily check in on aging parents. They could drop in and help out, run errands or take care of other issues that came up on short notice.\par \par \uc2 "You get with your siblings and develop a rotation," explained \uc2 John Pawlowski\uc2 , an analyst at \uc2 Green Street\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Greater flexibility to care for parents meant people delayed transitioning them to expensive senior-housing accommodations, as long as there wasn't an acute health reason to move them, Pawlowski said.\par \par \uc2 Markets with high levels of people working from home tend to have lower senior-housing occupancy rates, \uc2 Green Street\uc2  found. The three worst markets by occupancy, relative to before the pandemic, are San Jose, Calif., San Francisco and New York, known for higher levels of remote work. Senior-housing properties in the South and Southwest, where remote work is less common, tend to have recovered much better, \uc2 Green Street\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Other analysts say remote work could be having some effect but also point to other factors, such as the now-sputtering for-sale housing market. Many seniors have seen the value of their homes plummet from pandemic peaks, making some reluctant to sell and move into senior-living communities, said Vikram Malhotra, an equities analyst at \uc2 Mizuho Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The age at which people enter senior housing is also increasing, another sign that people are choosing to delay the transition. "People have decided post-Covid they want to age in place a little bit longer," Malhotra said.\par \par \uc2 The rising cost of senior living weighs heavily on that decision. The consumer-price index for nursing homes and adult day services rose 4.5% in May compared with one year ago, though the pace of this increase has lessened recently, alongside the drop in general inflation.\par \par \uc2 The median monthly cost of assisted living, specifically, was $4,500 in 2021, according to survey data from \uc2 Genworth Financial\uc2 , an insurance company. That is unaffordable to many American seniors, and a reason more developers are trying out new senior-living models that target lower price points.\par \par \uc2 Still, senior-housing operators say they are optimistic. They point to a historic surge in people turning 80 years old over the next few years, as well as the wealth this group has accumulated, relative to previous generations. Some operators are reporting stronger move-in activity this summer, said Bradley Schopp, executive director at Integra Realty Resources, a commercial-property-services firm.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, remote work arrangements are now winding down in some parts of the country.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230712ej7c0000z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
