\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Food-Delivery Apps Sue to Block N.Y. Minimum Wage Law\b0\par\par\uc2 By Preetika Rana \par \uc2 439 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 July 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , Uber Eats and \uc2 Grubhub\uc2  sued New York City on Thursday to block a new law that sets minimum wages for food-delivery workers, escalating a battle between the apps and the city over how the industry should be regulated.\par \par \uc2 The city recently passed a law that sets new pay rates for app-based delivery workers.\par \par \uc2 Starting July 12, the law requires that the companies either pay workers around 50 cents for every minute they spend on a trip, or pay them a minimum of $17.96 an hour for the time they spend active on each app. The latter works out to around 30 cents a minute. Both rates exclude tips.\par \par \uc2 Workers are currently paid a rate per trip that is set by each app. Tips are earned on top of that. \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , Uber Eats and \uc2 Grubhub\uc2  don't employ drivers, who are considered independent contractors or so-called gig workers.\par \par \uc2 The companies say the new per-minute rate on trips could end up being significantly higher than the minimum wage set for other industries.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, compensating workers for time spent online is flawed because delivery people are typically active on multiple apps at the same time and free to decline orders, according to the companies. They say the new calculus could lead to multiple apps paying a worker for the same hours.\par \par \uc2 The apps say the new metrics would force them to raise consumer fees in New York City to offset costs as well as limit the number of workers who could work for each app, hurting gig workers' flexibility.\par \par \uc2 "Delivery workers, like all workers, deserve fair pay for their labor, and we are disappointed that \uc2 Uber\uc2 , \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , \uc2 GrubHub\uc2 , and Relay disagree," New York City's Department of Consumer and Worker Protection, which drafted the law, said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "These workers brave thunderstorms, extreme heat events, and risk their lives to deliver for New Yorkers -- and we remain committed to delivering for them," the department added. "The minimum pay rate will help uplift thousands of working New Yorkers and their families out of poverty."\par \par \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  and \uc2 Grubhub\uc2  jointly filed a lawsuit in the Supreme Court of New York, while Uber Eats and Relay, another delivery company, each filed separately.\par \par \uc2 Food-delivery apps are battling New York City on other fronts.\par \par \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , Uber Eats and \uc2 Grubhub\uc2  sued the city in late 2021 after lawmakers capped the commissions apps could charge restaurants. \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  sued over another rule that required it to share more data with New York City restaurants. Both lawsuits are ongoing.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230707ej770000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
