\par\par \b \uc2 Pace of U.S. Job-Quitting Eases, Nearing Level Before Pandemic\b0\par\par\uc2 By Austen Hufford and Gwynn Guilford \par \uc2 1,015 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 July 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The surge in U.S. workers quitting their jobs has abated since peaking during the pandemic, another sign that the labor market is cooling from ultrahot levels as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raises interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Workers voluntarily left four million jobs in May, the Labor Department said Thursday. That marked a drop of around 500,000 from 4.5 million in November 2021, the highest level in Labor Department records back to 2000.\par \par \uc2 The quits rate -- the number of resignations as a share of total employment -- averaged 2.5% from March to May, down from 3% as recently as April 2022 and slightly higher than before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The decline in quits adds to underlying signs the labor market is easing, but top-line hiring remains firm. Employers added an average of 314,000 jobs a month this year, through May. Payroll-processing firm ADP estimated on Thursday that private-sector employers added 497,000 jobs in June. Economists surveyed by The Wall Street Journal estimate Friday's jobs report will show total payrolls rose by 240,000 last month.\par \par \uc2 The hiring figures reinforce the view that the Fed will need to raise rates further this year, which sent major stock indexes lower on Thursday. The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 366.38 points, or 1.1%, while the S&P 500 and Nasdaq Composite indexes also declined and Treasury yields increased following the ADP report.\par \par \uc2 Quitting rose as the economy recovered from job losses early in the pandemic. Many industries struggled with labor shortages as millions of people exited the job market. Some workers re-evaluated their lives and the kinds of jobs they wanted; others jumped ship for more money; others switched into fully remote roles. Those trends have since eased.\par \par \uc2 A slowdown in voluntary exits can indicate a softening labor market if it reflects employers easing demand for workers, economists said. Employees might have less confidence they will find a better job or feel they have less bargaining power. Others might just be content with their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Julius Maupins is staying at a Veritiv packaging and paper warehouse in Edwardsville, Kan., amid uncertainty about the broader economy. "I don't think right now is a good time to do a lot of jumping around," the 40-year-old said.\par \par \uc2 Veritiv\uc2  added perks to retain workers after they quit at higher rates early in the pandemic. The company boosted pay and benefits, including more vacation days, extra parental leave and profit-sharing bonuses. It also experimented with a four-day workweek. The turnover rate has declined, said \uc2 Dean Adelman\uc2 , the company's head of human resources.\par \par \uc2 Maupins recently took a vacation to Las Vegas with the extra days off. The new benefits make it "more likely that guys stay," he said.\par \par \uc2 Adelman said the extra benefits are worth it because losing an employee costs tens of thousands of dollars along with lost expertise, and it demoralizes those remaining. "High turnover perpetuates higher turnover," he said.\par \par \uc2 Quits touched the lowest level in more than two years in April before rising some in May, the Labor Department said Thursday. Employers sought fewer workers in May, with job openings falling to a seasonally adjusted 9.8 million from a revised 10.3 million in April.\par \par \uc2 Initial applications for unemployment benefits, a proxy for layoffs, rose last week and are up about 20% from the start of the year. The average number of hours worked a week has fallen, which in the past often preceded increased job cuts.\par \par \uc2 The economy more broadly appears to be moderating. Consumer spending cooled in May after a strong start to the year. Imports to the U.S., an indicator of expected demand, fell in May to the lowest level since late 2021, the Commerce Department said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 A cooling job market could help the Fed, which is trying to tame inflation by slowing the economy through higher interest rates. Those switching jobs command bigger raises, which can help fuel price increases. But their pay gains have ebbed recently.\par \par \uc2 Inflation and economic activity haven't slowed this year as much as Fed officials expected, prompting them to project more rate increases to come, according to minutes of their June meeting, released on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Workers are returning to the restaurant industry -- and sticking around, said \uc2 Greg Levin\uc2 , chief executive of \uc2 BJ's Restaurants\uc2 , a brewhouse with 216 locations. BJ's altered its training program to reduce departures. Now it caps how many new employees go through it at the same time.\par \par \uc2 "Things have really stabilized," Levin said. "Our retention is pretty much in-line with where they were in 2019."\par \par \uc2 The share of people who started a job and then got another within a year declined this spring, according to \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  data. An increasing slice of employees the professional networking site surveyed said it wasn't a good time to get or hold on to a job.\par \par \uc2 "The labor market has cooled from once-in-a-lifetime hot to a typical strong business cycle, very warm," said Guy Berger, an economist at \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some workers who landed big pay raises recently now plan to stick around.\par \par \uc2 Before the pandemic, Jason Crane worked as a pilot for a regional airline, making around $60,000 a year. The pandemic decline in air travel caused Crane to be assigned to a Denver airport, far from his home in North Carolina. In Denver, the 41-year-old would live for days at a time in a "crash pad" shared by nine other pilots.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, private plane operator flyExclusive hired him. He has since received a promotion and is on track to make around $180,000 this year. Crane said his lifestyle is much better, including knowing his work schedule for the entire year. He isn't looking for a new job because in a downturn, newly hired pilots are the first to go.\par \par \uc2 "Who knows when the next economic event is?" he said. "Your seniority is everything in this industry."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich451\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal6765
