\par\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Strike Hinges on Pride\b0\par\par\uc2 By Heather Haddon \par \uc2 599 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers disrupted operations at dozens of cafes this weekend and pledged to keep striking, citing concerns that include display of Pride decorations in stores and negotiations between unionized baristas and the coffee giant.\par \par \uc2 The company said it closed 21 stores early since Friday because of staffing shortages caused by the strikes. As of Sunday, 104 U.S. locations had staff participating in the strikes, in most cases limiting service to customers. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  owns and operates around 9,380 U.S. stores.\par \par \uc2 Baristas at Chicago, Baltimore, Seattle, Philadelphia and Pittsburgh stores were among those on strike, according to Starbucks Workers United.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, Starbucks Workers United accused the company of limiting the ability for workers to hang Pride decorations in stores. Managers told baristas that they didn't have enough work time to decorate, that it was a safety concern given some recent backlash against brands highlighting Pride, or that decorations didn't align with guidelines for store environments, according to the union.\par \par \uc2 Sean Plotts, a 26-year-old \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  worker who said he and other baristas in his Chicago store struck over the weekend, said his store didn't get to hang Pride decorations. "It's honestly a shame," he said.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's chief executive defended the company's commitment to the LGBTQ community and suggested advocates for the union were wrongly exploiting the situation. The company apologized to customers inconvenienced by the strikes and encouraged them to search for an operating cafe on its app.\par \par \uc2 "We strongly disapprove of any person or group, seeking to use our partners' cultural and heritage celebrations to create harm or flagrantly advance misinformation for self-interested-goals," said CEO \uc2 Laxman Narasimhan\uc2  in an internal company message. Partners is the company's term for employees.\par \par \uc2 The dispute highlights how tension can erupt when \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  gives stores discretion in applying its corporate guidance. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  sets overarching policies for its stores and lets local managers decide how to implement many of them.\par \par \uc2 The company gives discretion to store leaders as to how they decorate their stores across holidays and themes, as long as the approach isn't discriminatory against any one group. If a landlord doesn't allow for flags or signs, or a manager thinks they pose a safety problem, for example, then that store can choose to not have them.\par \par \uc2 The union, contending that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  changed its policies, filed a complaint earlier this month with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 , arguing that any prohibition in Pride decor should have gone through contract negotiations. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has denied it has changed its policies and said the union has relayed false information about its rules and negotiation efforts.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks Workers United said they plan to keep striking at stores through July 1. It said stores that had highlighted their intent to strike over the weekend received backlash. The union said it took down an online map that had listed striking stores out of safety concerns for workers.\par \par \uc2 The strikes are the latest skirmish between the company and pro-union workers in the nearly two years since Buffalo, N.Y.-area \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  employees petitioned to unionize. Hundreds of cafes followed in filing union petitions with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  had certified unions at 319 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  cafes and defeats at 65 locations, the agency said earlier this month. About 3% of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's U.S. stores have unionized. The pace of new union petitions has ebbed over the months. They fell last summer before rising again in March, then eased again this spring, \uc2 NLRB\uc2  records show.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230626ej6q0001q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
