\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Directors Guild Approves Contract With Movie, TV Producers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Flint \par \uc2 263 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Directors Guild of America\uc2  approved a new three-year labor agreement with Hollywood's major television and movie producers, a deal that comes as the writers' strike is showing no signs of abating.\par \par \uc2 The DGA said the contract was approved by a vote of 87%. Under terms, directors will receive an increase of 21% in royalties from streaming services such as Netflix. Much of that 21% will come from a significant jump in royalties from streaming services' international operations.\par \par \uc2 The agreement includes language to protect against the use of generative artificial intelligence, which has become a lightning rod in talks between directors, writers and performers and the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2 , which negotiates on behalf of studios, networks and streaming services.\par \par \uc2 The pact "ensures that DGA member's job duties will not be replaced by, or assigned to, GAI," the DGA said.\par \par \uc2 The deal directors approved Friday comes as the strike by TV and movie writers against the major studios, streamers and networks heads toward its third month. Talks between the writers and the AMPTP broke down in early May.\par \par \uc2 The Writers Guild of America may soon be joined on the picket line by the \uc2 Screen Actors Guild\uc2 , whose own deal with the AMPTP expires on June 30. Negotiations between SAG and the AMPTP have been going on for two weeks.\par \par \uc2 The AMPTP didn't issue a statement on the DGA accord. A representative for the Writers Guild didn't respond to a request to comment.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230626ej6q0001s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pic