\par\par \uc2 Help Wanted\par \b \uc2 No Degree Necessary\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stacey Guber and Jonathan Wolfson \par \uc2 445 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 June 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The doors of opportunity have opened a little wider in Georgia and Florida. They are the latest states to remove an unnecessary barrier to state jobs. Government employers can now hire any worker with the skills necessary to do a particular job, regardless of whether he has a college degree. Gov. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  signed Georgia's bill into law on April 27. Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed Florida's on Friday.\par \par \uc2 In the past year, governors in Colorado, Maryland, New Jersey, North Carolina, Pennsylvania, South Dakota, Utah and Virginia have taken executive action to filter prospective employees by skills, not degrees. This reform opens paths to opportunity and helps states fill jobs.\par \par \uc2 The problem of "degree inflation" is partially to blame for a workforce shortage in state government. Employers have for many decades used the college degree to screen candidates, even when a degree has no relevance to the job in question. Before Maryland's reform in 2022, Gov. \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 's team found that highly skilled military veterans were denied information-technology jobs with the state because they didn't have college degrees. Removing unnecessary barriers to employment and shifting to skills-based hiring helps qualified workers gain access to jobs for which they wouldn't otherwise be considered.\par \par \uc2 Georgia's new law also requires state agencies to conduct regular evaluations to ensure that such requirements don't creep back into job postings. An applicant with a micro-credential in project coordination and years of volunteer management for a local food pantry can qualify for a state project-management job without also completing a college degree.\par \par \uc2 Who stands to benefit? The nonprofit Opportunity@Work estimates there are 70 million STARs -- workers who have been "skilled through alternative routes." They include veterans, young computer programmers, and those who have taken time out of the workforce to raise children or care for a loved one at home.\par \par \uc2 This isn't a political issue. States led by Republicans and Democrats have passed reform. Businesses are also stepping up to mitigate degree inflation. In 2021, \uc2 IBM\uc2  and \uc2 Accenture\uc2  made significant steps toward skills-based hiring by building an apprenticeship pipeline and replacing degrees with skills on multiple job postings. In December 2022, \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  announced that most of its positions would no longer require a four-year degree.\par \par \uc2 A bill to remove degree requirements for state jobs is working its way through the Missouri Legislature. More states should join Georgia and Florida in making this vital reform permanent.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ms. Guber is a visiting fellow and Mr. Wolfson is chief legal officer and policy director at the Cicero Institute.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230620ej6k0000u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
