\par\par \b \uc2 Railroads Offer Improved Work Conditions --- Union Pacific, \uc2 CSX\uc2 , \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  shift tack in effort to ease staffing strains\b0\par\par\uc2 By Esther Fung \par \uc2 1,022 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 June 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Major U.S. freight railroads are adopting labor-friendly policies such as paid sick leave and predictable shifts to help address long-running staffing shortages that nearly boiled over into a nationwide strike last year.\par \par \uc2 The railroads have reached agreements with unions representing employees such as track workers and machinists to pay for at least four days of sick leave a year. Three companies -- Union Pacific, \uc2 CSX\uc2  and \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  -- also have signed agreements with unions to offer train crews more-predictable rest schedules.\par \par \uc2 The changes are in addition to providing unionized workers a retroactive pay raise under a new national contract as well as scheduled increases this year and in 2024.\par \par \uc2 These labor-friendly moves, company executives said, are aimed at boosting morale among existing workers and making jobs more attractive to those outside the industry. The four major U.S. freight railroads have increased the number of train and engine employees by 19% over the past three years, and staffing is nearing levels last seen at the start of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Short-term paid sick leave, which wasn't previously available for union workers, became a sticking point in contract talks last year between railroads and labor groups representing more than 115,000 employees.\par \par \uc2 As of late May, 57%of all union railroad employees had new paid sick-leave agreements in place, according to the \uc2 Association of American Railroads\uc2 , an industry trade group.\par \par \uc2 After months of tumult that resulted in interventions from Congress and the White House, railroad executives said they are ready for a reset in relations with workers.\par \par \uc2 "Having an engaged workforce and delivering strong returns to our investors are not mutually exclusive," said \uc2 Joe Hinrichs\uc2 , chief executive of Jacksonville, Fla.-based \uc2 CSX\uc2 , which became the first major railroad to reach a deal on paid sick leave with a union this year. "We can and will do both."\par \par \uc2 Companies in other industries such as ride-hailing and airline travel as well as food retailing and food manufacturing also have been evaluating ways to improve labor gaps beyond higher pay -- such as offering more-flexible shifts, more-comfortable uniforms and addressing other grievances.\par \par \uc2 Railroad workers have raised concerns for years about operating changes that companies pursued in a bid to become more efficient, labor leaders said.\par \par \uc2 Those changes often resulted in staff reductions, a problem that was magnified during the Covid-19 pandemic. Strong demand from rail customers and thin staffing levels left workers shouldering heavy workloads to keep trains moving.\par \par \uc2 A new labor pactlate last year didn't quell discontent among rank-and-file employees at several railroads. The companies continued to face pressure from politicians and union leaders to enhance workers' quality of life. They also fielded complaints from customers and federal officials to improve rail service, which they have associated with worker shortages.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders said they are now seeing a shift in how railroad operators are engaging with employees.\par \par \uc2 "America started to see that the cultures on these railroads were very toxic and it was time for a change," said Jeremy Ferguson, president of the International Association of Sheet Metal, Air, Rail and Transportation Workers -- Transportation Division, or SMART-TD.\par \par \uc2 Engineers on rail assignments at \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2  can be called to work anytime after completing their legally allocated hours of rest. But in late May, the Omaha, Neb., railroad and the \uc2 Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainmen\uc2  tentatively agreed to put workers on defined work schedules. In the new schedule, engineers would work no more than four days in a row and wouldget four consecutive days off in a 15-day period.\par \par \uc2 CSX\uc2  has a schedule of six work days and two rest days for its engineers, while \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  has a mix of five or six work days and two rest days.\par \par \uc2 "It's a huge step in the right direction," said Cliff Horton, a Sweetwater, Texas-based locomotive engineer at \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2 . Horton, who has been on the road crew for 10 years, said that workers currently aren't necessarily guaranteed time off because it is contingent on staffing levels.\par \par \uc2 "We will be able to schedule events. When you tell somebody you're going to be somewhere and you cancel on them, they just kind of give up on you," Horton added.\par \par \uc2 Higher employee wages combined with forecasts of paltry growth in rail volumes could curb profits for railroads this year, according to industry analysts. However, there are early signs that the companies' moves are resulting in higher staffing levels and retention, which help improve service for their customers, executives said.\par \par \uc2 "If we can be more stable and more consistent, there's a whole book of business out there from existing customers," said Union Pacific CEO \uc2 Lance Fritz\uc2  at an investor conference earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Freight railroads and unions aren't in lockstep on all potential changes in work rules, including those mandating the size of train crews.\par \par \uc2 In the current round of negotiations with conductors, Norfolk Southern and Union Pacific backed off efforts to reduce train crews to one person, an issue that workers have said could threaten rail safety and their job security. \uc2 CSX\uc2  didn't raise crew size issues in recent talks. SMART-TD, the union representing conductors, said it is still in talks with \uc2 BNSF\uc2  on crew-size requirements.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 BNSF\uc2  is in continued dialogue with all our unions on modernization concepts and ways to address quality of life," said \uc2 BNSF\uc2  spokesman Zak Andersen.\par \par \uc2 Railroad operators have said in recent years that improved technology has rendered the role of an onboard conductor obsolete, and that conductors could be redeployed to other positions that would minimize time away from home.\par \par \uc2 For now, \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  is giving priority to predictable work assignments and time off for conductors, as well as wellness benefits such as paid sick leave, said Thomas Crosson, a company spokesman.\par \par \uc2 "To give us the space to negotiate those changes and implement them, we stepped back from mandatory conductor redeployment negotiations at this time, though voluntary discussions remain an option," Crosson said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich488\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7320
