\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Heard on the Street: A Still-Soaring Labor Market Fights the Fed --- Weakness in the job numbers has yet to materialize\b0\par\par\uc2 By Justin Lahart \par \uc2 576 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 June 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 The job market keeps singing a tune from Bizarro Annie: "The sun will go down tomorrow, just you wait and see."\par \par \uc2 For now, though, the sun is still shining. The Labor Department on Friday reported that the economy added a seasonally adjusted 339,000 jobs last month, far more than the 190,000 economists polled by The Wall Street Journal expected. The already solid employment figures from the prior two months were also revised up by a total of 93,000.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate rose to 3.7% from May's superlow 3.4%, but it is difficult to see this as evidence that the job market is slowing. The unemployment rate is based on a separate survey of households, with a smaller sample size than the employer survey the jobs figures are based on. Not only do the household survey's employment figures tend to be more volatile, the decline they registered in May came from areas not covered by the employer survey, such as people who said they were self-employed or worked in private households. Take those away, and the household-survey measure goes from showing a decline of 310,000 people employed to a gain of 394,000.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the gain in the headline jobs figure was driven by increases in service-sector areas where staffing levels appear to still be short of prepandemic norms. The leisure and hospitality sector added 48,000 jobs, while healthcare added 52,400. And employment in goods-producing sectors continues to hold up: The number of manufacturing jobs edged down by 2,000, while the construction sector added 25,000.\par \par \uc2 One message from the report is that the banking troubles kicked off by the collapse of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  in March still don't appear to be putting too much of a dent in the job market. While the decline in the number of the self-employed might indicate people are having a harder time securing loans to get their ventures off the ground, established employers don't seem to be retrenching.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, before the report came out, some forecasters thought the May figures might be depressed by temporary factors. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economists, for example, thought that with the labor market tight and many young people still in school, businesses that increase employment in the summer would struggle to hire. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  economists thought that the factors the Labor Department uses to smooth seasonal swings would push the job count lower. That the May employment report showed such strong gains despite those hurdles suggests the June report, too, could be very strong.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  policy makers, for their part, still seem likely to keep rates on hold when they meet later this month, perhaps taking a bit of cover from the rise in the unemployment rate. But with debt-ceiling fears now gone, and the risk that banking-sector problems will bleed the economy looking diminished, the central bank's focus will stay squarely on inflation that is still too high and a job market that is running far stronger than it would like. By July, policy makers could be raising rates all over again.\par \par \uc2 The job market hasn't met its kryptonite yet, but that doesn't mean the Fed can't deliver it.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich465\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6975
