\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Union Sabotage at the Supreme Court\b0\par\par\uc2 584 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 June 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Are striking workers protected when they deceive an employer into making inventory that they intend to ruin? Readers might be shocked that this is a live question, and on Thursday the Supreme Court said 8-1 that the answer is no. \uc2 Glacier Northwest\uc2  v. \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  is worth highlighting because it's another example of the wild things unions believe they're entitled to do.\par \par \uc2 Glacier Northwest\uc2  sells concrete in Washington state. The company alleges, as Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  writes for the majority, that the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  intentionally called a work stoppage "when the Union knew that Glacier was in the midst of mixing substantial amounts of concrete, loading batches into ready-mix trucks, and making deliveries." Sixteen truck drivers refused to complete their deliveries. Nine abandoned their trucks without a word, threatening damage if the concrete hardened inside.\par \par \uc2 What ensued, Justice Barrett continues, was "a mad scramble," in which "over the course of five hours, nonstriking employees built special bunkers and managed to offload the concrete." The trucks were saved, but the product was a loss, and Glacier sued the union in state court. The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  claimed the drivers' conduct was "arguably protected" by the federal National Labor Relations Act (NLRA) of 1935, and the Washington Supreme Court agreed.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  majority says a longstanding legal interpretation is that such lawsuits are not pre-empted when strikers "fail to take 'reasonable precautions' to protect their employer's property from foreseeable, aggravated, and imminent danger." The union argued that workers in the meat and milk industries have had their right to strike upheld, even though walking off the job can create a risk of spoilage.\par \par \uc2 Justice Barrett zeroes in on a material difference: "Given the lifespan of wet concrete, Glacier could not batch it until a truck was ready to take it. So by reporting for duty and pretending as if they would deliver the concrete, the drivers prompted the creation of the perishable product. Then, they waited to walk off the job until the concrete was mixed and poured in the trucks." Far from defending the alleged destruction, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  should be embarrassed by it.\par \par \uc2 Five Justices are represented by the majority opinion. The textualist troika of Justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  cement the same outcome using different legal reasoning. The lonely dissenter is Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , who wants the courts to pause their review so that the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  can first consider the dispute. According to the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 's general counsel, Justice Jackson says, the argument that the strike was protected "appears to have merit."\par \par \uc2 That's a testament to what the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  lets unions get away with. In its 2016 \uc2 Cooper Tire\uc2  decision, the board said picketing Ohio tire workers couldn't be punished by the company for yelling racist remarks at replacement staff, including: "Go back to Africa, you bunch of -- ing losers!" That legal interpretation was reversed under President Trump, but the Biden Administration has now reversed the reversal. Bigotry is apparently fine as long as it's unionized.\par \par \uc2 Union membership in the private economy keeps dropping, with no end in sight, and this type of behavior is part of the story. The labor organizer's fantasy is that every strike is like \uc2 Disney\uc2 's "Newsies," a tale of plucky salesboys in 1899 who demand better terms from New York's miserly press barons. The court filings always read more like "On the Waterfront."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230602ej620001i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
