\par\par \b \uc2 Office Districts Suffer, Neighborhoods Boom\b0\par\par\uc2 By Konrad Putzier and Kate King \par \uc2 920 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 May 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 While office towers sit empty and nearby businesses struggle to pay their bills, residential neighborhoods in the U.S.'s biggest cities are bustling again.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic and remote work have done little to dent the overall appeal of cities such as New York, Chicago and Los Angeles, foot-traffic and rent data show. Instead, the pandemic has shifted the urban center of gravity, moving away from often sterile office districts to neighborhoods with apartments, bars and restaurants.\par \par \uc2 "We're now back to what cities really are -- they're not containers for working," said Richard Florida, a specialist in city planning at the \uc2 University of Toronto\uc2 . "They're places for people to live and connect with others."\par \par \uc2 At the height of the pandemic, some analysts predicted that big cities would enter a downward spiral as remote workers sought more space and cheaper places to live. That happened to some degree early on, but it didn't last. While big metropolitan areas lost population during the first year of the pandemic, partly because of a drop in immigration from abroad, the losses have since slowed or reversed, according to a \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2  analysis of census data.\par \par \uc2 Many residential neighborhoods benefit from remote work. As people spend more time at home, they frequent local shops, gyms and restaurants, boosting the economy of places such as Brooklyn, N.Y.'s Ditmas Park and Williamsburg, as well as Washington, D.C.'s Georgetown.\par \par \uc2 Data from Placer.ai, which tracks people's movements based on cellphone usage, shows a stark divide between office and residential districts. In Downtown Los Angeles, visitor foot traffic is 30.7% below prepandemic levels, while Downtown Chicago's visitor foot traffic is 27.2% lower. By contrast, in the residential areas of South Glendale and Highland Park near Los Angeles and in Chicago's residential Logan Square neighborhood, visitor foot traffic has been rising and is nearly back to prepandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 Food delivery also illustrates the shift. In 2019, almost 95% of New York City corporate lunch orders came from the city's business district, according to food-order app \uc2 Grubhub\uc2 . This year, it is down to around 85%. In Chicago, the central business district accounted for more than 80% of corporate lunch orders in 2019 but just over 60% this year.\par \par \uc2 Rent data, meanwhile, attests to strong demand for city living. In Manhattan's Greenwich Village, median housing rent was 30% higher in April 2023 than in April 2019, according to \uc2 Jonathan Miller\uc2 , chief executive of real-estate-appraisal firm Miller Samuel. In the Brentwood neighborhood of Los Angeles, the median rent is up 63%.\par \par \uc2 Big cities still face serious challenges. Vacant office buildings leave downtown shops and restaurants with too few customers, while falling commercial building values threaten property-tax revenues.\par \par \uc2 "The increased vibrancy of great urban neighborhoods will never be enough to offset the decline in property-tax revenues caused by remote work and the falling values of commercial office buildings," Florida said.\par \par \uc2 Housing shortages have pushed up rents. In the long run, replacing offices with apartments can help revitalize urban centers, but that will take time. Conversions are also often tricky and expensive. Crime is up in many places. San Francisco in particular has been slower to recover and its retail has come under pressure.\par \par \uc2 Still, anyone walking through New York's Jackson Heights or Silver Lake in Los Angeles looking for a deserted hellscape won't find one.\par \par \uc2 In Manhattan, the pandemic ignited a retail renaissance in the Soho neighborhood, with availability there now at its lowest level since 2014, according to real-estate services firm \uc2 Cushman & Wakefield\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Before the pandemic there was a disconnect between landlord expectations and what tenants could pay," said Steven Soutendijk, executive managing director for the firm's retail division. "Covid sort of shook that up a little bit, in a good way."\par \par \uc2 Andrea Loscalzo, owner of the Italian restaurant Salumeria Rosi in Manhattan's Upper West Side, said his eatery is as busy as before the pandemic. Many regulars left the neighborhood and never returned, but young professionals in their 30s and 40s moved in to replace them, he said.\par \par \uc2 "Even as families decamp, New York's magnetic pull on the young and the talented is now more than ever," Florida said.\par \par \uc2 In Chicago's central business district, retail vacancy rose to a record high of 28% last year compared with about 15% in 2019, according to Stone Real Estate, a local brokerage. Crime in the city remains a concern, and in April, \uc2 Walmart\uc2  said it would close four of its eight locations in Chicago after annual losses nearly doubled in five years.\par \par \uc2 The city's residential and tourist neighborhoods are performing considerably better. In River North, which has a mixture of residential, office and hotels, retail vacancy dropped by more than 2 percentage points, driven largely by the strength of its restaurants, said John Vance, principal at Stone Real Estate.\par \par \uc2 "The city blocked off some streets to traffic so we could have expanded outdoor dining," Vance said. "River North feels vibrant."\par \par \uc2 Lakeview, a neighborhood within walking distance of Lake Michigan and Wrigley Field, is bustling with young residents, families and Cubs fans, said resident Naomi Polinsky. Its restaurants and bars were packed on a recent Saturday night.\par \par \uc2 "We walked next door to the sports bar, and there was not a single place to sit. We walked across the street to the wine bar, completely crowded," she said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich252\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal3780
